WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.065 --> 00:00:02.190
PRIYANKA VERGADIA: Welcome
to Get Cooking in Cloud,

00:00:02.190 --> 00:00:05.530
where we share the best recipes
to apply in your cloud kitchen.

00:00:05.530 --> 00:00:07.360
I'm Priyanka and
in this episode,

00:00:07.360 --> 00:00:09.870
I am pleased to
welcome back Jen Person

00:00:09.870 --> 00:00:13.359
to talk about another mobile
app backend service solution.

00:00:13.359 --> 00:00:19.716
[THEME MUSIC]

00:00:23.140 --> 00:00:25.212
Hello, Jen, welcome to
Get Cooking in Cloud.

00:00:25.212 --> 00:00:26.420
JEN PERSON: Thanks, Priyanka.

00:00:26.420 --> 00:00:29.050
I'm super excited to
share today's solution.

00:00:29.050 --> 00:00:30.490
I think it's a real winner.

00:00:30.490 --> 00:00:32.073
PRIYANKA VERGADIA:
Well, so far you've

00:00:32.073 --> 00:00:34.000
shown us some really
great recipes.

00:00:34.000 --> 00:00:36.970
We've looked at some
architectures using Firebase,

00:00:36.970 --> 00:00:39.400
and then another one using
Firebase and App Engine

00:00:39.400 --> 00:00:40.360
standard.

00:00:40.360 --> 00:00:43.030
And then yet another one
using Firebase and App Engine

00:00:43.030 --> 00:00:44.570
flexible environment.

00:00:44.570 --> 00:00:47.420
So we're all wondering what
are we cooking up today?

00:00:47.420 --> 00:00:49.660
JEN PERSON: Well, the
ingredients for today's recipe

00:00:49.660 --> 00:00:52.090
are the App Engine
standard environment

00:00:52.090 --> 00:00:53.845
and the Endpoints frameworks.

00:00:53.845 --> 00:00:54.970
PRIYANKA VERGADIA: Oh, yes.

00:00:54.970 --> 00:00:57.280
I know that Endpoints
framework for the App Engine

00:00:57.280 --> 00:01:00.400
standard environment generates
APIs, client libraries,

00:01:00.400 --> 00:01:03.100
and discovery documentation
for an App Engine app.

00:01:03.100 --> 00:01:04.330
JEN PERSON: Exactly.

00:01:04.330 --> 00:01:06.610
This means you don't have
to write wrappers to handle

00:01:06.610 --> 00:01:08.440
communication with App Engine.

00:01:08.440 --> 00:01:11.050
And with the client libraries
generated by Endpoints,

00:01:11.050 --> 00:01:14.590
you can make direct API
calls from your mobile app.

00:01:14.590 --> 00:01:16.450
Using Endpoints with
App Engine gives you

00:01:16.450 --> 00:01:19.570
an app platform that
monitors, updates, and scales

00:01:19.570 --> 00:01:20.570
the hosting environment.

00:01:20.570 --> 00:01:22.070
PRIYANKA VERGADIA:
What sort of apps

00:01:22.070 --> 00:01:23.902
might not be a
great fit for this?

00:01:23.902 --> 00:01:26.110
JEN PERSON: Well, I don't
recommend this architecture

00:01:26.110 --> 00:01:29.500
if your app requires automatic
real time data synchronization

00:01:29.500 --> 00:01:30.850
across devices.

00:01:30.850 --> 00:01:33.640
For those apps, you'll want to
see one of the solutions that

00:01:33.640 --> 00:01:35.035
includes Firebase.

00:01:35.035 --> 00:01:36.910
PRIYANKA VERGADIA: So,
in Priyanka's kitchen,

00:01:36.910 --> 00:01:39.243
could you help me understand
this with an example?

00:01:39.243 --> 00:01:40.660
JEN PERSON: Well,
remember when we

00:01:40.660 --> 00:01:43.630
talked about updating
player scores and rankings?

00:01:43.630 --> 00:01:46.210
Since all client devices
needed the updated scores,

00:01:46.210 --> 00:01:48.520
one of our previous
solutions using Firebase

00:01:48.520 --> 00:01:49.872
is better for that use case.

00:01:49.872 --> 00:01:52.330
PRIYANKA VERGADIA: So, what
are some of the other apps that

00:01:52.330 --> 00:01:54.185
are not a great fit?

00:01:54.185 --> 00:01:56.560
JEN PERSON: So, if you're
backend services require custom

00:01:56.560 --> 00:01:58.750
servers or third
party libraries,

00:01:58.750 --> 00:02:01.000
you'll want to look at
a solution with Firebase

00:02:01.000 --> 00:02:02.843
an App Engine
flexible environments.

00:02:02.843 --> 00:02:03.760
PRIYANKA VERGADIA: Ah.

00:02:03.760 --> 00:02:06.790
Like for example, if I needed
custom payment processing

00:02:06.790 --> 00:02:10.430
server to process payments
in Priyanka's Kitchen app,

00:02:10.430 --> 00:02:12.490
then that payment
processing server can

00:02:12.490 --> 00:02:14.890
be on App Engine flexible
environment integrating

00:02:14.890 --> 00:02:16.150
with Firebase, right?

00:02:16.150 --> 00:02:17.650
JEN PERSON: Exactly.

00:02:17.650 --> 00:02:20.495
And finally, if your
systems don't support SSL,

00:02:20.495 --> 00:02:22.120
you'll want to choose
another solution,

00:02:22.120 --> 00:02:25.133
because SSL is
required by Endpoints.

00:02:25.133 --> 00:02:27.550
PRIYANKA VERGADIA: And if App
Engine with Endpoints sounds

00:02:27.550 --> 00:02:29.650
like a good solution
for developers,

00:02:29.650 --> 00:02:31.365
then how can they find out more?

00:02:31.365 --> 00:02:32.740
JEN PERSON: Linked
below is a set

00:02:32.740 --> 00:02:34.390
of getting started
tutorials that

00:02:34.390 --> 00:02:37.210
show how to build the
Hello Endpoints sample app.

00:02:37.210 --> 00:02:39.670
This sample lets you
send simple messages.

00:02:39.670 --> 00:02:42.320
The backend service is
implemented on App Engine

00:02:42.320 --> 00:02:44.545
and exposed to the mobile
app with Endpoints.

00:02:44.545 --> 00:02:45.670
PRIYANKA VERGADIA: Awesome.

00:02:45.670 --> 00:02:48.940
Well, if your use case requires
making direct API calls

00:02:48.940 --> 00:02:50.740
from the mobile
app, then it's worth

00:02:50.740 --> 00:02:54.010
checking out cloud Endpoints
and try this recipe.

00:02:54.010 --> 00:02:56.140
That's all for today on
Get Cooking in Cloud.

00:02:56.140 --> 00:02:58.660
Jen, thank you so
much for being here.

00:02:58.660 --> 00:03:00.610
JEN PERSON:
Absolutely, it was fun.

00:03:00.610 --> 00:03:03.113
And if you'd like to know
more about today's recipe,

00:03:03.113 --> 00:03:05.530
you can check out the Endpoints
with the App Engine guides

00:03:05.530 --> 00:03:06.322
and reference docs.

